    <article class="content-guide-section">
      <div class="container guide-container">
        <section class="content-card visual-card" aria-labelledby="label-heading">
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">Read the bag</p>
            <h2 id="label-heading">Volume buys coverage. Pounds plan the carry.</h2>
            <p>A lava rock bag can be labeled in cubic feet, pounds, or both. Use the <strong>net volume</strong> for the coverage calculation. Use the product's <strong>actual labeled weight</strong> to plan pickup or delivery. The two figures serve different jobs.</p>
            <p>For example, <a href="https://www.quikrete.com/dealers/products/products-hardscapes.asp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">QUIKRETE lists a 0.5 ft³ red lava rock bag</a>, while one <a href="https://www.lowes.com/pd/EARTHESSENTIALS-BY-QUIKRETE-0-5-cu-ft/50437588" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">retail listing for a 0.5 ft³ bag states 18 lb</a>. Weight depends on the specific stone, grading and product, even when bag volume is the same.</p>
          </div>
          <figure>
            <img src="/lava-rock-bag-label.svg" alt="Illustrated lava rock bag: use the 0.5 cubic foot volume for coverage and the separate product weight for loading." width="800" height="480" loading="lazy" />
            <figcaption>Illustration of label fields to check; not a product package.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section class="content-card" aria-labelledby="method-heading">
          <p class="eyebrow">Worked order</p>
          <h2 id="method-heading">A 10 × 10 ft bed, two inches deep</h2>
          <div class="formula-box">
            <code>Net ft³ = area (sq ft) × depth (in) ÷ 12</code>
            <code>Planned ft³ = net ft³ × (1 + extra % ÷ 100)</code>
            <code>Whole bags = round up (planned ft³ ÷ labeled ft³ per bag)</code>
          </div>
          <p>For 100 square feet, 100 × 2 ÷ 12 = <strong>16.67 ft³</strong>. Divide by a bag labeled 0.5 ft³: 16.67 ÷ 0.5 = 33.33, so buy <strong>34 whole bags</strong>. You receive 17 ft³ in those bags. If the site calls for 10% extra, plan 18.33 ft³ and buy <strong>37 bags</strong>. Apply the allowance before rounding.</p>
          <p>One 0.5 ft³ bag covers <strong>3 sq ft at 2 inches</strong> or <strong>2 sq ft at 3 inches</strong> by volume; the <a href="https://www.flowerocklava.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FloweRock manufacturer chart</a> gives the same 2-inch yield. For other depths or bag sizes, let the calculator divide the volume.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="content-card" aria-labelledby="store-heading">
          <p class="eyebrow">Before checkout</p>
          <h2 id="store-heading">Make the bag count usable at the store</h2>
          <ol class="buying-list">
            <li><strong>Match the actual package.</strong> Read the cubic feet on the bag, then check stone size and color against the material you want. If only pounds are listed, ask for cubic-foot yield or coverage at a stated depth before estimating area.</li>
            <li><strong>Measure the finished layer.</strong> Split curved beds into smaller measured shapes and subtract pavement or other openings. Larger pieces can leave visible gaps in a thin layer; choose a depth that fits the material and site instead of treating two inches as a guarantee.</li>
            <li><strong>Check weight and price separately.</strong> Multiply whole bags by the product's labeled pounds and local per-bag price. As a product-specific illustration, 34 bags labeled 18 lb equal 612 lb of bagged product. Check your vehicle's own cargo limit with passengers and tools included.</li>
            <li><strong>Compare a delivered bulk quote for larger jobs.</strong> Fifty-four 0.5 ft³ bags equal one cubic yard by volume. Compare your bag total and transport with a local quote for the same rock and enough loose volume, including delivery and any minimum. The <a href="/bulk-vs-bagged-rock">bulk versus bagged guide</a> helps with that decision.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="content-card" aria-labelledby="placement-heading">
          <p class="eyebrow">Where the rock goes</p>
          <h2 id="placement-heading">Choose the layer for the place, then calculate it</h2>
          <p>Bagged lava rock is useful for small decorative beds, dry landscape accents and narrow areas where loose delivery is awkward. A <a href="/lava-rock-calculator">lava rock project estimate</a> covers broader yardage and weight planning; the <a href="/lava-rock-coverage-calculator">lava rock coverage calculator</a> answers how far a quantity on hand will spread.</p>
          <p>Rock mulch does not decompose to improve soil. It can also warm soil in summer, and dust and leaves eventually support weeds between stones, even over landscape fabric. <a href="https://yardandgarden.extension.iastate.edu/how-to/using-mulch-garden" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Iowa State Extension</a> describes these limits; <a href="https://ipm.ucanr.edu/home-and-landscape/weed-management-in-landscapes/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">UC IPM</a> notes that fabric under rock can separate stone from soil but dark rock may stress plants. Keep leaves out of the bed and watch plants near sun-heated stone.</p>
          <p class="safety-note"><strong>Gas fire pit?</strong> Landscape package coverage does not set a burner fill depth, and <a href="https://www.homedepot.com/p/100427379" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">a common 0.5 ft³ landscape bag is expressly marked unsuitable for fire pits</a>. Choose only media approved for your appliance. <a href="https://hpcfire.com/wp-content/product-documents/Indoor-Outdoor-Product-Instructions/Fire%20Pit%20Inserts/Outdoor%20-%20Push%20Button/Fire%20Pit%20Insert%20-%20Push%20Button%20%28820%29.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HPC's burner instructions</a> say to cover the ring only as directed and leave ignition vents open; <a href="https://cms.firegearoutdoors.com/documents/All-instructions/90214A-FPB-BS-Outdoor-Square-Pan-Flat-Disc-Fire-Pits-with-All-Weather-System-AWS-Installation-and-Operating-Instructions.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Firegear's manual</a> warns that wet media can crack or pop.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="content-card faq-card" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
          <h2 id="faq-heading">Lava rock bag questions</h2>
          <div class="faq-list">
            {faqItems.map(item => (
              <div class="faq-item">
                <h3>{item.q}</h3>
                <p>{item.a}</p>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>
    </article>

    <TopicClusterNav
